1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,400
In 2019, a cotton seed
sprouted on the Moon.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:07,200
Then the lunar night came,
and it did not survive.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,200
This is Alo.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,600
Can your crew keep her alive
through one Moon night?

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,300
Real missions are trade-offs. The power
that warms the crew also makes their air.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,400
The water that shields them from a
storm is the water the plants drink.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,800
Most space lessons skip that,
or make it too hard to follow.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,700
Our challenge: Build a Junior
Astronaut Mission Trainer,

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,400
where students run a Moon outpost,

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,800
and feel which choices make
a mission succeed or fail.

11
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,600
This is Last Light Academy.

12
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:47,400
You and three friends run Aurora Station,
at the Moon's south pole.

13
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,600
The Sun is about to set
for thirty-six hours.

14
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,400
Four cards stay on screen: life support,
radiation shielding, power and food.

15
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,600
Everything you do moves them.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,200
At the Mission Table,
you plan every shift of the night.

17
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,200
Kai knows the battery, Amara the air,
Mei the garden, Rafi the space weather.

18
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,800
Nobody sees everything.

19
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,600
Press Try it, and the forecast shows what
will happen before anything changes.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,400
Making air now uses sunlight
that a full battery would waste.

21
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:32,600
Lock it in, and the station
lives with your choice.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,600
Then the last light goes,
and the solar masts stop.

23
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:39,900
In this training scenario,
the link to Earth is cut.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,400
No help from Mission Control.

25
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:56,400
Shift five.
Rafi's radiation counter jumps.

26
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:01,800
A solar storm: a replay of the
real one from 10 September 2017.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,200
Seventy minutes of warning.

28
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:07,600
Pump water into the shelter wall,
stack supplies, or wait?

29
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,200
Every choice costs something.
Water in the wall protects the crew,

30
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,600
but it is the same water Alo's roots need.

31
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,400
Every number has a Data Card.

32
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,700
This storm curve was measured
by NOAA's GOES-13 satellite,

33
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:32,600
with flare times from
NASA's DONKI records.

34
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,800
Each value is labelled: observed,
computed, or a training assumption.

35
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,200
At sunrise you see what you chose,

36
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,200
what the forecast said,
and what really happened.

37
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:48,800
Keep the crew safe and Alo alive,
and at first light she blooms.

38
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:54,200
NASA data sits at the core of the game.

39
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:58,800
Twenty-nine NASA and partner sources
ship inside it, and it works offline.

40
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,800
Air comes from NASA's
life support baseline:

41
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:05,400
0.895 kilograms of oxygen
per person per day.

42
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:10,400
Power, from NASA Langley's ten-kilowatt
solar array for the south pole.

43
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:16,000
Shielding: about twenty centimetres of
water, from NASA radiation studies.

44
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,200
The ground is real LRO
LOLA elevation data,

45
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:24,600
and the Sun and Earth move as
JPL Horizons computes them.

46
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,600
It's free, open source,
and runs offline on Windows and Mac.

47
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:34,600
Up to four players share one screen,
each with private clues.

48
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:38,400
We are Team The Last BrainCell,
from Dhaka, Bangladesh:

49
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:43,400
Nafis Mohtasim Ramim, Khandaker Shadman
Sakib Siddiquee, Md. Asif Iqbal,

50
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:48,600
Abu Sayem Mohammed Tanvir,
Shahariar Rahman Shanto and MD Atik Khan.

51
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,000
AI-made assets are credited on screen.

52
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:55,000
One night. Four friends.
The first flower on the Moon.
